<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eberhard of Franconia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 885–<a href="/years/939">939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eberhard was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> from 918, the brother of King Conrad I. He carried the royal insignia to Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, supporting his election as king in 919, but later rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s son Otto I. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-andernach">Battle of Andernach</a> in 939, after which Franconia came directly under the crown.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eberhard was the second son of Conrad the Elder and his wife Glismut, probably an illegitimate daughter of the Carolingian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a>. His family, the Conradines, were counts in the Franconian Lahngau and loyal supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>, and they competed for power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> with the Babenberg sons of Duke Henry of Franconia. In 906 the two families fought near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a>: Conrad the Elder was killed, as were two of the three Babenberg brothers, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a> then took the Conradines' side.</p><p>After Louis died in 911 and Eberhard's brother was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> as Conrad I, Eberhard appeared from 913 as count in the Hessengau and Persgau and in the Upper Lahngau. He supported his brother against the rival dukes Arnulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and in 914 took the office of a Franconian margrave, though he could not stop Henry's conquest of the Thuringian lands of the late Duke Burchard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a>, the dying Conrad persuaded Eberhard to give up any claim to the crown and to urge the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> to choose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, seeing this as the only way to end the feud between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. Eberhard handed over the royal insignia at the diet held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritzlar">Fritzlar</a> in May 919, then served King Henry I loyally. After Henry reconquered Lotharingia he made Eberhard its regent in 926, and Eberhard ruled there until 928, when Henry enfeoffed his son-in-law Gilbert.</p><p>Under Henry's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>, who sought to strengthen royal authority, relations broke down. In 937 Eberhard besieged Helmern Castle near Peckelsheim, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> burgrave refused fealty to any non-Saxon. At a royal court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, Otto fined Eberhard and sentenced his lieutenants to carry dead dogs in public, a dishonouring punishment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 938 Eberhard rebelled with Otto's half-brother Thankmar and Duke Eberhard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The revolt was soon put down: Thankmar was killed at Eresburg Castle and the Bavarian duke replaced by his uncle Berthold. After a brief reconciliation, Eberhard joined Duke Gilbert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, Archbishop Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and Otto's brother Henry in a new uprising, which ended in their defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andernach">Andernach</a> on 2 October 939. Eberhard was killed there, allegedly by his Conradine relative Count Odo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> remained a direct imperial possession until 1039.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eberhard of Franconia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Andernach on the Rhine, followers of King Otto I of Germany decisively defeated rebels led by <b>Eberhard of Franconia</b>, the brother of the late King Conrad I. The rebel leaders were killed, and Franconia came directly under the crown.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-2">2 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 911 Conrad was elected king of East Francia as Conrad I, the first non-Carolingian ruler, while his brother <b>Eberhard</b> governed Franconia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-franconia">Duchy of Franconia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andernach">Andernach</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.44,7.4&amp;year=920" aria-label="Andernach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4719 411 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Eberhard of Franconia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5169 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5169 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.44,7.4&amp;year=920">What was here in 920 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
